<SiteLayout title="Job Application Tracker" eyebrow="Project 02">
  <section class="page-hero compact"><div class="kicker">Open source / Self-hosted / For job seekers</div><h1>A better place to<br /><em>track the search.</em></h1><p class="lead">A job application tracker that is simple enough for everyday use, but thoughtfully built under the hood.</p></section>
  <p class="pill">Concept · planning</p><div class="interlude"></div>
  <div class="page-content prose"><h2>The problem</h2><p>Once a job search gets busy, it becomes surprisingly easy to forget where you applied, which résumé you used, and whether two different links lead to the same requisition. People deserve an understandable way to manage that information without depending on a large recruiting platform.</p><h2>The small first release</h2><ul><li>Applications, companies, interviews, contacts and follow-up dates.</li><li>A clear status timeline with notes and links to original listings.</li><li>Duplicate detection using normalized job identifiers and database constraints.</li><li>CSV import/export and a responsive interface.</li><li>One-command local deployment with Docker Compose.</li></ul><h2>What I'm studying through it</h2><p><strong>Core:</strong> Java 21, Spring Boot, PostgreSQL, validation, migrations, automated tests, REST API design and secure deployment defaults.</p><p><strong>Correctness:</strong> transaction boundaries, concurrent submissions, unique constraints, and optimistic locking when multiple sessions edit the same record.</p><p><strong>Later, optionally:</strong> background reminders and Kafka-backed events, introduced only where an asynchronous workflow adds useful behavior.</p><div class="notice">This page describes a planned application, not shipped functionality. Demo and source links will be published once a usable version exists.</div><p><a class="text-link" href="/projects/">← Back to projects</a></p></div>
</SiteLayout>
